/**
\mainpage Yoyo development

Yoyo is a highly versatile graphical user interface to create dynamic user interfaces to calculate,
display and communicate values.

The yoyo data model is a tree structure with subtrees dedicated to different tasks. There is a
subtree dedicated to data objects, one which represents the widgets to display. There are optional
subtrees for the purpose of communication and serialization/deserialization.

The data model can be enriched at run-time using the yoyo-sdk - a plugin interface.

\section main_build How to build Yoyo

First step is to satisfy the dependencies:

- CMake 3.21 or greater
- Qt 5.15
- Boost 1.78 or greater
- Doxygen (optional)

You can download and install GoogleTest, if you want, but it is not necessary, since CMake will take
care of that, if you didn't.

It is recommended to use ninja-build to schedule the compilation steps, because it is much faster
than make.

On Linux systems install the necessary dependencies using the system's package manager.

\subsection main_build_creator Build from Qt Creator

When opening the project for the first time, Qt Creator will ask you to configure the project.

- Choose one of the following compilers (if applicable): gcc, minGW, clang (not tested with MSVS)
- Select 32-bit or 64-bit (both supported)
- Verify, that ninja is known to Qt
- Verify Qt version and CMake version
- Modify the CMake initial parameters to enable the use of ninja and to help CMake find Boost (Win
    only)
  - CMAKE_MAKE_PROGRAM:STRING=C:/path/to/ninja/ninja.exe
  - CMAKE_PREFIX_PATH:STRING=%{Qt:QT_INSTALL_PREFIX};C:/path/to/install/of/boost/boost_1_78

Configuration should be successful at that point. Click on build to start compiling.

\subsubsection main_build_creator_test Run tests

After the project is configured successfully, a list of executables (including test executables)
is available when selecting the build configuration (monitor-like icon on the lower left pane). Here
you can select an individual test to be executed. The play buttons (shortcuts: CTRL-R or F5) will
start the selected executable.

If you want to run all tests at once, you'll need to add a "custom executable" item in the "Run"
configuration on the "Project" page of the creator:
- binary: path to ctest.exe (next to cmake.exe)
- working directory: the build directory

Select this custom executable in the configuration selection and run it.

\subsection main_build_cl Build from command line

For the sake of simplicity, let's assume the Yoyo source code is located in \c SOURCE and the build
artefacts should go to folder \c BUILD.

\warning Do not perform in-source-builds (\c BUILD equals \c SOURCE). This can lead to unwanted
    effects on the source control management.

\subsubsection main_build_cl_win Windows version

We need some more definitions for Windows systems here:

- \c QT_DIR : path to your installation of Qt (not Qt 5.15, but the parent directory)
- \c CC : holds the path to the C-compiler binary; usually something like \c %QT_DIR%/Tools/mingw810_32/bin/gcc.exe
- \c CXX : holds the path to the C++-compiler binary; usually something like \c %QT_DIR%/Tools/mingw810_32/bin/gcc.exe
- \c NINJA_EXECUTABLE : holds the path to the ninja executable. If installed with Qt, this could be
    \c %QT_DIR%/Tools/Ninja/ninja.exe
- \c BOOST_DIR : the installation directory of the (built) boost libraries

\code{.bat}
cd %BUILD%

cmake.exe %SOURCE% -GNinja \
                   -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Debug \
                   -DCMAKE_PROJECT_INCLUDE_BEFORE=%QT_DIR%/Tools/QtCreator/share/qtcreator/package-manager/auto-setup.cmake \
                   -DCMAKE_CXX_COMPILER=%CC% \
                   -DCMAKE_C_COMPILER=%CXX% \
                   -DCMAKE_MAKE_PROGRAM=%NINJA_EXECUTABLE% \
                   -DCMAKE_PREFIX_PATH=%QT_DIR%/5.15.2/;%BOOST_DIR%

cmake.exe --build .
\endcode
Running the tests is rather straight forward on the command line
\code{.bat}
ctest.exe
\endcode
\subsubsection main_build_cl_linux Linux version

On Linux you can also choose the compilers to use by setting the \c CC and \c CXX environment
variables, or passing the path to the compiler binaries with the \c CMAKE_CXX_COMPILER and
\c CMAKE_C_COMPILER parameters, we'll omit that here.

If you used the package manager to install ninja, boost and Qt, the command reduces to:
\code{.sh}
cd $BUILD

cmake $SOURCE -GNinja -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Debug

cmake --build .
\endcode

Running the tests is rather straight forward on the command line
\code{.sh}
ctest
\endcode

\section main_installer Building Installer

\subsection main_installer_win Installer for Windows
On Windows, CMake doesn't support packaging, so we need to use a separate tool to create the actual
installer. The choice had been Inno Setup (https://jrsoftware.org/isdl.php).

\todo There exists already a installer creation configuration file (installer/yoyo.iss.in), but
    since there is no Yoyo application yet, the actual build has not been enabled. Also, the license
    file is still missing.

\subsection main_installer_linux Installer for Linux

CMake on Linux provides CPack, which is capable of creating packages for various Linux package
managers.

\todo Currently there is no packaging for Linux packages defined!

\code{.bat}
cd $BUILD
cmake $SOURCE -GNinja -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release
cmake --build .
cpack .
\endcode

*/
